// RFC-0006 §3.4 / §3.5, the two promises `byard dev` made and did not keep:
// the last good view stays behind a blurred error overlay, and a reload held
// behind an in-flight gesture says so instead of looking broken.
//
// This example exists to be *broken on purpose*. It is a scene with enough
// colour and structure that you can tell whether it is still there behind the
// overlay, and a drag handle you can hold to make the gate fire.
//
// Run it (from the repo root):
//   cd crates/byard-cli/examples/error_overlay
//   cargo run -p byard-cli -- dev
//
// ── 1. THE BLURRED BACKDROP (RFC-0006 §3.4) ───────────────────────────────
//
// With the window open, introduce a **parse** error, delete a closing brace,
// or truncate the file mid-attribute, and save. (An *unknown attribute* is a
// semantic error found during lowering, not a parse error, so it is reported by
// `byard check` rather than by the overlay; §3 below is where you see that one.)
//
//   • THE SCENE IS STILL THERE. Blurred, darkened, desaturated, but the
//     layout, the colours and *where you were* all survive. That was the
//     promise; the implementation painted an opaque field instead, and the
//     comment explaining why was honest and correct at the time, the flat
//     four-pass encoder drew all text in one global pass after every box, so
//     the app's text bled over the scrim. Z-layers and backdrop blur removed
//     that constraint.
//
//   • NOTHING UNDERNEATH IS READABLE. That is the other half of the design: a
//     legible background competes with the error text for the same attention.
//     You should be able to see *that* your app is there and not read a word
//     of it.
//
//   • THE PANEL DOES NOT FILL THE WINDOW. It floats, so the overlay reads as
//     something in front of the app rather than instead of it.
//
//   • THE CENSUS SAYS THE VIEW IS STILL THERE. Watch the `N boxes` field on
//     the statusline as the overlay mounts: it *rises*, the app's boxes plus
//     the backdrop pane plus the error panel. Under the old implementation it
//     collapsed to one, because the app was not drawn at all. That is the
//     single cheapest confirmation that this promise is being kept, and it
//     does not depend on your eyes.
//
//   • THE MOUNT AND THE DISMISS ARE BOTH CLEAN. Watch the edges carefully as
//     the overlay appears, and again as you fix the file and it goes away.
//     Neither transition may leave a band of stale pixels anywhere. This is
//     the one to look at: the encoder's scissor union is computed from what
//     changed between two frames, and these are the two frames where the whole
//     composition changes at once. Both ask for a full redraw; nothing in
//     between does.
//
//   • FIX IT AND SAVE. The overlay goes; the scene comes back sharp, with its
//     state intact, the counter below has not reset.
//
// ── 2. `↻ pending` (RFC-0006 §3.5, the gate) ──────────────────────────────
//
// A structure-incompatible reload cannot be applied mid-gesture: remounting a
// tree under a pointer that is holding one of its elements would drop the
// gesture on the floor. So it waits. Before this, it waited *silently*, and a
// developer holding a drag while a save queued behind the gate saw an
// application that had simply stopped responding to their edits.
//
//   • Press and HOLD the mouse button on the drag strip below.
//   • While holding, save a structure-incompatible change, add or remove a
//     whole element, e.g. delete the `Text("hold me")` line.
//   • The statusline shows `↻ pending` in amber. Nothing else changes: the
//     gate is doing its job, and now it says so.
//   • Release. The reload lands, `↻ pending` clears in the same step, and the
//     reload count beside it increments.
//
// The indicator and the gate are one type in the implementation for exactly
// this reason, an indicator that can be left on after the reload landed, or
// off while one waits, is worse than no indicator.
//
// ── 3. CARET DIAGNOSTICS (RFC-0006 §C3) ───────────────────────────────────
//
// The same broken file, checked from the terminal:
//
//   cargo run -p byard-cli -- check crates/byard-cli/examples/error_overlay
//
//   error is printed as:
//     main.byd:12:15: error[UnknownAttribute]: unknown attribute `bgg` …
//       |
//    12 |         Box #[bgg: 0x6750A4] {}
//       |               ^^^
//
// The first line is byte-identical to what it has always been and carries no
// escapes at all, even with `CLICOLOR_FORCE=1`, editor problem matchers parse
// it, and breaking one makes an integration go *quiet* rather than fail. The
// colour is on the block beneath it. `--short` drops the block entirely, for
// scripts:
//
//   cargo run -p byard-cli -- check --short crates/byard-cli/examples/error_overlay
View Main() {
    var drags = 0

    // `on pressed` is the engine's live interaction state, so the strip
    // visibly reacts while you hold it, which matters here, because holding
    // it is the whole instruction.
    let strip = style {
        bg: 0x2B2930, radius: 12, p: (vertical: 22, horizontal: 24), color: 0xE6E1E5
        on hover   { bg: 0x36323C }
        on pressed { bg: 0x4A4458 }
    }

    Column #[bg: 0x101014, p: 28, gap: 20, width: 760] {
        Text("RFC-0006, the dev runner's three promises")
            #[color: 0xFFFFFF, size: 24, weight: bold]
        Text("Break this file and save. The scene should still be here, blurred, behind the overlay.")
            #[color: 0x9AA0AC, size: 13]

        // Deliberately colourful and structured: a blurred field of one flat
        // colour would prove nothing about whether the view is really behind
        // the overlay.
        Row #[gap: 12] {
            Box #[width: 150, height: 90, radius: 14, bg: 0x6750A4] {}
            Box #[width: 150, height: 90, radius: 14, bg: 0x386A20] {}
            Box #[width: 150, height: 90, radius: 14, bg: 0xB3261E] {}
            Box #[width: 150, height: 90, radius: 14, bg: 0xE8B931] {}
        }

        Box #[bg: 0x1B1B22, radius: 12, p: 18, width: 704] {
            Column #[gap: 8] {
                Text("Readable text, so you can check you cannot read it through the blur.")
                    #[color: 0xCCD2E0, size: 15]
                Text("If any of this is legible behind the overlay the backdrop is too weak: a readable background competes with the error text for the same attention.")
                    #[color: 0x938F99, size: 13]
            }
        }

        // ── the hold target, for the `↻ pending` gate ─────────────────────
        //
        // Press and HOLD here, save a structure-incompatible change, and watch
        // the statusline rather than the window. The reload waits behind the
        // gate for as long as the pointer is down.
        Button("hold me, then save a structural change") #[..strip, width: 704]
            => drags++

        Text("{drags} gesture(s) completed, and the count survives every reload")
            #[color: 0x938F99, size: 13]
    }
}
